  "articleBody": "It's frustrating when your Google Business Profile seems to be invisible, right? You've put in the effort, but local customers just aren't finding you. Often, this boils down to a few key areas that might be overlooked, impacting your eligibility for local search visibility.\n\nFirst up, make sure your profile is complete and accurate. Google really emphasizes having all your ducks in a row – business name, address, phone number, website, hours, and services. Incomplete profiles just don't get the same love. Next, double-check your categories; are they truly the most accurate and fewest possible for your business? Google frowns upon stuffing categories or picking ones that don't quite fit, and it can actually hurt your visibility.\n\nAnother big one is verification. You can't make significant changes or unlock full visibility until your profile is verified. If you're having trouble here, Google might be rejecting edits or even questioning the business's real-world representation. Also, be honest about your address. If you're a service-area business, don't display a storefront address if customers don't actually visit you there – that's a common policy violation. Lastly, are your reviews managed well? Artificial or incentivized reviews can trigger policy issues, which Google is pretty strict about.\n\nWhen Professional Guidance Helps\nSometimes, things get tricky, especially if verification is stuck, your profile is suspended, or you're managing multiple locations. That's when a pro who understands Google's policies can really help. They can navigate disputes, resolve duplicate listings, and ensure policy-sensitive edits are handled correctly, keeping your business in Google's good graces and visible to local searchers.",
